That only works if the same directory they're in also happens to
be the current directory.  For a lot of things that is the case,
but when you're building software you generally want the current
directory to be your build directory, and you don't want that
littered with all your build tools.

PATH management on Windows can be a real pain, because of the way
directory structures are normally handled on that platform.

It was worse back when a lot of software was still in widespread
use that had been originally written for Windows 95 or 98 (or,
heaven help you, Windows 3.x), which did not have any such
concept as file or directory ownership, and then "updated" to run
on NT by making the minimum number of changes that would get it
to actually run, without the proper from-scratch rewrite that was
very much needed in almost every instance.  Back then, it was
common to have an application's configuration and user-preference
data reside in the same directory as the program executable.
Fortunately, these days, most of that has cleared up.

But you're still left with needing to add a directory to the PATH
for each and every utility or application that is installed on
the system (well, technically, you only need a PATH entry for
ones you intend to use from the command line, plus all the ones
that any other programs that you are using require).

My path here (on a Debian system) has five directories in it.  In
those five directories there are almost three thousand executable
programs.  /usr/bin alone has 2693.  This has disadvantages, as
well as advantages.  One disadvantage is that if you ever have to
use a file manager or open-file dialog box to browse to the
location of a particular program for some reason, it takes much
longer than it would on Windows.  (Yes, that's something I've had
to do on numerous occasions; two examples include needing to
inform a web browser what helper app to use to open a particular
file and needing to start up a window manager when there isn't
one currently running.)  However, one advantage, and this is a
rather significant thing for anyone who uses the command line at
all, is that managing your PATH environment variable is a
veritable piece of cake.

(Another advantage, in theory, is that if your filesystem
supports automatic versioning that can be turned on and off at
the directory level you can easily turn it on for things like
/etc and /home and leave it off for things like /usr/bin and
/var/log.  Unfortunately, this is rather moot.  Even though
automatic versioning has been around and has worked very well in
fringe operating systems since practically forever, it has yet to
be properly implemented in any major operating system, unless you
consider VMS to be major.  Even in VMS, you couldn't turn it on
and off at the directory level, although that's at least partly
because of the esoteric manner in which VMS handled directory
hierarchies and paths.)

So anyway, yeah, if you try to do serious computing on Windows
you typically need a whole collection of batch files that exist
solely to set up your PATH environment variable in an appropriate
way for various kinds of tasks, because there isn't any one way
you can set it that will work for everything.  You can't just
list every directory that has executable files in in that you
might want to call from the command lien, because there's a limit
on how large environment variables can be, and it falls well
short of listing a thousand or more directories.  Sometimes even
for individual tasks you end up needing to use SUBST to create
shorter paths so that everything you need will fit.  Admittedly,
most of my experience in this regard is with Win9x, so it's
possible that NT improves the situation somewhat, but if so I am
not aware of it.

